<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Orwell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Eric Arthur Blair, Orwell</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Orwell, the pen name of Eric Arthur Blair, was an English novelist, essayist, journalist and critic whose work opposed all totalitarianism and supported democratic socialism. Born in Motihari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, he joined the Indian Imperial Police in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1922 and later fought for the Republican side in the Spanish Civil War, where he was wounded in the throat. The Road to Wigan Pier (1937) and Homage to Catalonia (1938) drew on such experiences. He is best known for the allegorical novella Animal Farm (1945) and the dystopian novel Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949), whose words such as Big Brother and doublethink entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-orwell.jpg" alt="George Orwell" width="330" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Branch of the National Union of Journalists (BNUJ). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Orwell_press_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eric Arthur Blair was born on 25 June 1903 in Motihari, Bengal Presidency, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, into what he described as a lower-upper-middle-class family. His father, Richard Walmesley Blair, worked in the Opium Department of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>, and his mother, Ida Mabel Blair, grew up in Moulmein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. When he was one year old his mother took him and his sister Marjorie to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in 1904 she settled at Henley-on-Thames. Apart from a brief visit in mid-1907, he did not see his father until 1912.</p><p>He attended a convent school in Henley run by French Ursuline nuns, and then, through a scholarship, St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprian">Cyprian</a>'s School in Eastbourne from September 1911. He hated the school and later wrote the essay Such, Such Were the Joys about it, published after his death. There he met Cyril Connolly. Later he won a scholarship to Eton. As a child he took a keen interest in natural history, in butterflies, birds and fishing, and his adult diaries are full of observations of nature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Blair wrote under the pen name George Orwell. For most of his career he was best known for journalism, in essays, reviews, columns and books of reportage: Down and Out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, The Road to Wigan Pier (1937), on the living conditions of the poor in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and Homage to Catalonia (1938), on his experiences soldiering for the Republican side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. He later called that war his watershed political experience, and said that everything he had written since 1936 was directed against totalitarianism and for democratic socialism.</p><p>Modern readers meet him more often as a novelist, through the allegorical novella Animal Farm (1945), often thought to reflect the degeneration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>, and Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949), on life under totalitarian rule. Many of his coinages, such as Big Brother, Thought Police, Room 101, Newspeak and doublethink, entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>. He was an atheist who identified with the humanist outlook, yet he took part in the life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. His works are widely studied in schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and his political legacy is contested, with critics on both left and right claiming or attacking him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Orwell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1944 <b>George Orwell</b> observed that as used, the word had become almost entirely meaningless.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>George Orwell</b> distinguished nationalism from patriotism and called it “power-hunger tempered by self-deception”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>George Orwell</b>, in Notes on Nationalism (1945), characterised pacifism as a form of nationalism and wrote that most pacifists belonged to obscure religious sects or were humanitarians who preferred not to follow their thoughts further; he also claimed that a small group of pacifist intellectuals were moved by…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert Service, Dmitri Volkogonov and others contend that he shared the same totalitarian strand of thought; <b>George Orwell</b> doubted that there was much difference.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/trotskyism">Trotskyism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>…was the theme of the North Korean film The Flower Girl (1972), whose music was claimed to be the work of Kim Il-sung, and in <b>George Orwell</b>'s Animal Farm (1945) the tune of &quot;Beasts of England&quot; is described as something between &quot;La Cucaracha&quot; and &quot;Oh My Darling, Clementine&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a></li><li><blockquote>Secker and Warburg published <b>George Orwell</b>'s novel of a totalitarian surveillance state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Motihari, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.65,84.92&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Motihari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of George Orwell"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4612 5278h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4612 5278h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.65,84.92&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
